Fixed and verified. Here's what was actually wrong.

## Root cause

Not a permissions problem, not PEP 668, not a `PATH` mixup. Your pip **source tree had been deleted out from under its own metadata**:

- `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` was present and intact, `RECORD` and all.
- `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/` — the actual package — did not exist.

So every entry point died on the first line it executes:

```
File "/usr/local/bin/pip", line 5, in <module>
    from pip._internal.cli.main import main
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'
```

Both `pip` and `python3 -m pip` were affected, because the console script and the `-m` route import the same missing package.

Worth knowing what it *wasn't*: the machine has a genuinely `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` system Python at `/usr/bin/python3.11`, but your `python3` is a source-built 3.13.1 under `/usr/local` that that marker doesn't cover. If you'd gone looking for that error message you'd have found a real one on this box and fixed the wrong interpreter.

## The fix

`python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` — the obvious repair — **did not work**, and this is the part worth remembering:

```
Requirement already satisfied: pip in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages (24.3.1)
```

`ensurepip` checks the dist-info and concludes pip is installed. It never looks at whether the files are there. That no-op would have left you believing you'd fixed it.

What did work: extracting the wheel `ensurepip` already carries offline, `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`, directly into site-packages. No network was needed or used.

## Verification

```
pip --version              → pip 24.3.1 from .../site-packages/pip (python 3.13)
pip3 --version             → pip 24.3.1 from ...  (same)
python3 -m pip --version   → pip 24.3.1 from ...  (same)
pip list                   → pip 24.3.1
python3 -m pip debug       → sys.platform: linux, certifi CA bundle resolved, DEBUNDLED: False
```

`pip debug` matters more than the `--version` lines: it exercises the platform and vendor machinery, so this is a whole pip, not a stub that prints a version string.

## Two things to be aware of

**This fix lives in the container layer and will vanish on rebuild.** The root cause is that something *deleted* `site-packages/pip/` while leaving its metadata — a truncated layer, an overzealous cleanup step, a `pip` install interrupted mid-write. Fixing the live filesystem treats the symptom; if this is a Dockerfile-built image, add a layer that installs pip properly, or the next rebuild reproduces it.

**Don't take pip's own advice here.** `pip list` suggests `pip install --upgrade pip` (24.3.1 → 26.3). Network is disabled by policy on this machine, so that will just fail — and it's how a partial install gets interrupted in the first place. Stay on the bundled 24.3.1 until you can reinstall deliberately.

One caveat on my own reach: my tool scope here has no shell, so the diagnosis and repair ran through the `implementor` role's `bash`. The commands and outputs above are that role's, and the verification commands are the ones you can re-run yourself in one line to confirm it independently.

